# Phase 18 MessageExt body override follow-up — 2026-10-21T18:41:00Z

Branch: `phase/18-phase-18-compatibility-closure`
Code revision: `81d67a5e` (`feat(common): dispatch MessageExt subtype bodies`)
Pinned Java source: `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`.
Fixture SHA-256: `9b67a09bc850192e2856bc908c10a6dfa6590db3f924b32fe62b6e97edcc510f`.

## Source audit and change

- Java `ProcessQueue.putMessage` reads virtual `MessageExt.getBody()` to
  calculate cached bytes. Rust previously resolved this inherited method
  through the base `Message` value even when a caller-defined subtype supplied
  an override.
- Added `MessageExtSubtype` hooks for `getBody` and `setBody`. `MessageExt`
  returns an opaque live read view for the stored base body or a runtime subtype
  snapshot for the override; nullable reads preserve Java null. The concrete
  read-view type is hidden behind `impl Trait`, so this does not add a public
  concrete type to the facade.
- The invoked Java fixture now records custom body length in direct-consume
  callbacks and exercises the subtype through `ProcessQueue.putMessage`.
  Rust compares callback length, retained byte size and setter count. All 27
  direct-consume cases and the ProcessQueue fixture comparison pass.
- Updated the Phase 6, Phase 10 and Phase 18 crosswalks and release checklist.
  Mutable custom body-array aliasing and other inherited virtual methods remain
  open.

